message: If you use this repository, please cite the manuscript.
title: "Applicability domains as testable claims: predictive uncertainty against molecular similarity for metal–ligand selectivity"
authors:
  - family-names: TBD
    given-names: TBD
type: article
license: TBD
repository-code: "https://github.com/nochemi2k/bnn_extraction"
abstract: >
  An applicability domain is usually drawn as a region of resemblance to the
  training set, computed from the molecule alone, and the same notion decides how
  benchmarks are split. This work recasts it as a testable claim: a criterion earns
  standing only when the predictions it calls safe prove more accurate than an
  equally sized random selection, judged on a population the criterion did not
  itself choose. Applied to metal-ligand selectivity under ligand-disjoint
  validation, the standard similarity criteria fail that test while the model's own
  predictive uncertainty passes it, cutting held-out error by two thirds at half
  retention and raising the yield of a one-ligand experiment from 64% to 83%.
